  "notes_for_editor": "Disagreements stated in text: tower height (NPS 90 ft, LighthouseFriends just over 86 ft, record 27.0 m), electrification (late 1930s vs May 1942), diaphone (1925 vs 1929), fog-signal houses combined (1900 vs 1901). USLHS article names the 1874 assistant John Drouillard, roster says John Dullard; not merged. The NRHP nomination's statement that the lights were automated in the 1940s conflicts with 1961. LighthouseFriends is the only source for much of the construction narrative, the 1905 log and Anna Carlson's interview, which are quoted as that page prints them. The Light-House Board reports for 1873-74 are not in the corpus, so the construction story rests on LighthouseFriends. Bulletin 1924-29 index lists 'keepers stranded by ice' at page 225 of the cumulative series; the issue was not found. Roster data matched from the USLHS page; Mart Allwardt is the only listed USCG man. Current characteristic Fl W 10s and range in the record were not verified in a cited source, so are omitted. The 1913 Bulletin PDF is cited as printed by corpus.py cite. The Omaha Bee and SF Call give different distances from shore (600/800/500 ft) for the capsized yawl; not used. Carlson's later move to Michigan Island is from LighthouseFriends (1893).",
